<Base title="Why separate the view from the work? — Evan Han"><article class="article"><a class="back" href="/#notes">← Back to field notes</a><div class="eyebrow">DESKTOP / WPF / MVVM</div><h1>Why separate the view from the work?</h1><p class="notice">An independent learning note with a hypothetical monitoring interface.</p><p>In an equipment monitoring application, the screen may change often while the underlying communication logic needs to remain understandable and testable. MVVM provides a useful way to separate these concerns.</p><h2>Give each part a purpose</h2><p>The view describes presentation. The view model exposes state and commands for that view. Services and models handle the underlying data and device interaction. Data binding connects the interface to observable state without making the view responsible for network details.</p><h2>Design for real states</h2><p>Show loading, disconnected and error states as deliberately as the happy path. Use commands that reflect whether an action is available, and update bound properties on the appropriate UI thread.</p><h2>Test the decisions</h2><p>A view model can be exercised with a simulated service: verify that a disconnect changes the displayed status, that commands respond to state, and that errors are surfaced clearly.</p></article></Base>

